From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:05:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add the Xiaomi Book S 12.4

Add the Xiaomi Book S 12.4 (TM2133), a Snapdragon 8cx Gen 2 (SC8180XP)
detachable. Its DSDT is Qualcomm's CLS board with board ID 1, so the
modem subsystem reports CLSB8180 and the WCN3990 integrated in the SoC
does Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. It is closest to the Lenovo Flex 5G, whose
DSDT has the same USB-C, UFS, USB and PCIe PHY descriptions, so the tree
follows the Flex 5G's and changes what this DSDT gives differently.

The GPIO interrupts in this DSDT use GIO0 virtual pins. Pin 64 * n is
the nth interrupt in GIO0's _CRS, and the PDC routes that interrupt from
one GPIO. Read that way, the lid event (pin 320) comes out as GPIO 121,
the pin GIO0 reads as LIDR, and the Flex 5G's touchpad and PCIe 3 wake
as GPIO 24 and 180, which its tree uses.

The DSDT gives these values.

- The touchscreen (HIMX1234, \_SB.TSC1) is HID over I2C at 0x4f on the
  QUP at 0x884000 (i2c1) at 400 kHz, with its descriptor at 0x1 and its
  interrupt on GPIO 122, level low. The PEP powers it from LDO4_C at
  3.3 V and LDO12_E at 1.8 V and drives GPIO 54 high around them, which
  the tree treats as an active-low reset.
- The lid, the keyboard cover's magnet, reads GPIO 121.
- UCS0 lists the Flex 5G's USB-C pins, orientation on GPIO 38 and 58
  and the SBU muxes on GPIO 152 and 100 and on 188 and 187, so the
  pmic-glink connectors and muxes are the Flex 5G's.
- STOR is 0, so the UFS controller is off and the drive is NVMe.
  PRP0, PRP2 and PRP3 enable PCIe 0, 2 and 3. Their reset GPIOs are 35,
  175 and 178 in QPPX, CLKREQ is on 36, 176 and 179 in the PEP, and
  ports 0 and 3 wake on pins 384 and 576, GPIO 37 and 180.
- The modem subsystem's WLAN power resource is on for board ID 1, and
  the PEP powers WLAN from LDO1_E and Bluetooth from LDO7_A, LDO9_A and
  LDO11_C, the Flex 5G's rails. The Flex 5G's second chain supply
  (LDO10_C) is not in this DSDT and is left out.
- The GPU SMMU's context interrupts are GSIV 712 to 719, edge
  triggered, as the Inspiron 14 3420's tree has them.
- Xiaomi's modem subsystem INF installs qcmpss8180_nm.mbn for
  CLSB8180.

The regulators the touchscreen adds take the PLDO's 3.296 to 3.304 V
range for 3.3 V, which the PLDO's 8 mV steps can't hit exactly. From the
Inspiron 14 3420's tree (also SC8180XP) come the 3.07 and 3.15 GHz OPPs
of the prime cores, the GPU SMMU interrupts and the RTC offset in UEFI.
The memory carveouts and rmtfs are the Flex 5G's.

The tree leaves these out.

- The display. The panel config names a 1600x2560 DSI panel with DSC,
  PNC357DB1-4, whose init sequence unlocks a Himax HX83121A. Linux's
  HX83121A driver knows only the BOE and CSOT PPC357DB1-4 panels, so
  MDSS stays off and the screen on the firmware's framebuffer, and
  DisplayPort over USB-C stays off with it.
- The keyboard cover's connector (TXRA9530 at 0x3b on i2c7) and the
  device RHPX exposes at 0x48 on i2c1, which have no Linux driver.
- The wake GPIO of PCIe 2. Its pin 512 reads as GPIO 177, but the
  Galaxy Book S DSDT, which names its pins directly, puts PCIe 2's wake
  on GPIO 104.
- Audio, the cameras, the volume keys and the SAR sensor.

The firmware loads from qcom/sc8180xp/Xiaomi/book-s. The ADSP, modem
and WLAN images are signed under a Xiaomi root, the CDSP and GPU zap
shader images under Qualcomm's QMC Attestation Root CA 3. Not booted on
the laptop yet.

Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
